Original commit body from 68eb8023c592f8cf32b6a034f26e638c377a7314:

Ten subcommands run a full analysis on a cache miss through one shared
chokepoint, _get_or_run_analysis, which passed no side-emission flags at all.
So a caller who typed `slice --files` also paid for three budget-tier previews,
up to 25 per-route handler slices, and the sketch pre-computation block --
while _handle_files_mode reads Symbol.path/id and Edge.src/dst and nothing else.

--minimal declines the set. Measured on this monorepo, cold run_survey:

    default     220.0s
    --minimal   160.9s     (-59.1s, -26.9%)

OPT-IN, DEFAULTS UNTOUCHED, per the owner ruling on WI-bikod and the standing
steer on the adjacent surface (WI-pijal: "don't jettison any of those files").
The change is not that the artifacts stop being worth writing; it is that a
command which never asked for a survey can now say so. Not offered on `survey`
itself, which already has --budgets / --no-sketch-fan-out / --no-handler-slices;
that boundary is pinned by a test rather than left to convention.

Behavioral evidence, both signs, on two tiny repos so each run was a real cache
miss:

    default    3 tier previews, slices dir present, sketch_precomputed present
    --minimal  0 tier previews, slices dir absent,  sketch_precomputed absent

Both arms returned the SAME answer (2 dependent files) -- non-destruction
asserted, not merely absence. And `sketch` run against a --minimal cache exits 0
with a complete sketch, so the documented degradation (recompute, not
incorrectness) is verified rather than inferred from reading the four guards.

THE PARITY TEST DERIVES ITS POPULATION rather than hardcoding ten names: it
walks the real parser, follows each subparser's `func` default to the handler,
and asks whether that handler calls the auto-analysis chokepoint. A future
consumer cannot silently miss the flag. A companion control asserts the
derivation selects a non-empty set, since a broken derivation would make the
parity assertion vacuous.

smart-test passes --minimal to its reverse slice, but CAPABILITY-PROBED. The
slice runs through the pipx $STABLE_HG, which lags the working tree by design
and did NOT know the flag on the development machine. An unrecognized argument
exits non-zero, and the slice-failure branch routes to run_full_suite -- so
blind adoption would have converted every local run into a ~90-minute full suite
while still reporting success. A --help probe costing ~0.2s gates it. The
shipped block is executed against advertising and non-advertising stubs with a
positive control asserting the two answers differ, plus a static guard that the
probe's answer reaches the slice invocation and not merely the diagnostic
string. The invocation stays on one line because three existing guards scan
smart-test line-by-line and by proximity -- they fired on my continuations, and
the fix was to satisfy them, not loosen them.

Also lands scripts/measure-analyzer-scaling.py, which prices the follow-on work:
the analyzer phase is 65.7s uncapped but 2.6s at max_files=1, so fixed cost is
under 4% and the phase is ~96% per-file. It surfaced two hazards, filed as
WI-fomid (max_files is documented as a per-language cap but only python and
javascript honour it -- 252 of 1385 files still analyzed at a cap of 1, which
made a linear fit return an impossible negative intercept) and WI-nuvam
(duration_ms is per-pass WALL under a ThreadPoolExecutor, so it inflates with
contention: the same 43 bash files billed 2.3s or 49.9s depending only on what
python was doing alongside, which reads as a pathologically slow analyzer and is
not one).

CLI flag-matrix baseline regenerated: exactly 10 commands gained exactly
--minimal, nothing else moved.

check-package-coverage core: 100% in isolation. Full suite 5,634 passed, all
files at 100%, mypy ratchet clean.

